THE F WORD
  F carries an 8-bit word over the superframe, so it pins BIT phase
  and SUPERFRAME phase with one check.  mfspec used 1010, which
  spec.md 3.3 lists as "imitated by CVSD silence" and mf-vs-16bit 4.1
  measured passing 100% of windows on an idle channel.

  Longest run of consecutive frames a payload lane can match the word,
  over every superframe phase.  Acquisition needs 32 in a row, so any
  lane whose longest run is under 32 is rejected DETERMINISTICALLY.

  word                              CVSD speech CVSD silence   constant 1   constant 0       random
  10101010  period 2 -- mfspec             1369         8192            1            1           12
  11101110  period 4 -- spec.md 3.3 T            4            3            3            1           14
  11111110  one zero in eight                 4            3            7            1           20
  11111100  two zeros, adjacent               5            2            6            2           14
  11101100  five ones, period 8               7            3            3            2           14

  0xAA is matched indefinitely by CVSD silence -- it IS CVSD silence.
  0xEE has period 4, so it cannot pin an 8-state superframe at all.
  0xFE, 0xFC and 0xEC all reject silence and constants outright.
  0xFE is chosen because its generator is the cheapest: F = 0 only at
  state 7, so TX F = NAND(q2,q1,q0) -- one 3-input gate, and the
  receiver forms the same term from its own counter.

  A WRONG SUPERFRAME PHASE at the right bit alignment is rejected
  deterministically too: 11111110 rotated against itself mismatches
  at 2 of every 8 frames, so the longest good run is 6 and 32
  consecutive is unreachable.  That is what mfspec had no answer for.

