SUPERFRAME PHASE -- can a wrong one be held?

  mfspec: the only thing pinning the phase was two pads in the S lane,
  4 apart, so the check only ever compared channels 4 apart and any
  pair differing in hook state admitted a wrong phase -- 56 of 64.

  mf8: F itself carries the phase.  At offset d the receiver expects
  11111110 and sees it rotated by d, which mismatches at 2 frames in
  8 for every d != 0.  Hook states cannot enter into it.

   offset   frames matching of 8   longest good run
        0                      8                 64
        1                      6                  6
        2                      6                  5
        3                      6                  4
        4                      6                  3
        5                      6                  4
        6                      6                  5
        7                      6                  6

  Every wrong offset tops out at 6 consecutive good frames, against
  the 32 acquisition needs.  Rejected deterministically, for every
  hook state, with no enumeration required -- but here it is anyway:
  wrong superframe phases admissible over all 64 hook states: 0

GUARD POSITION -- why 0 and 4

  False locks over all 64 hook states x 8 skews (512 per cell).  "one
  data card" is the F word in ONE seat, voice elsewhere -- legal under the
  slot rule, so it has to be 0 in every seat for the rule to mean anything.
  "voice" is how often speech at nominal level satisfies the guard 4
  superframes running, which is what a chance acquisition on the residual
  case (`mf8.py seats` 3) needs.

  layout                        F word, every lane  one data card, worst seat        voice
  0,4 = 0,1  (this spec)                   0 / 512                    0 / 512     1 in 499
  3,5 = 1,0  (before 3.4)                  0 / 512                    0 / 512    1 in 1200
  3,7 = 0,1  (regular)                     0 / 512                    0 / 512     1 in 459
  3,7 = 1,0  (mfspec values)             192 / 512                    0 / 512     1 in 484

  Any two same-parity states with different values reject CVSD idle, which
  gives the same value at both.  mfspec's values copy the F word at 3 and 7,
  so they add nothing.  Every other layout is clean; what separates them is
  cost and the residual.  0,4 = 0,1 decodes as enable = NOR(q1,q0),
  expected = q2 -- one gate, no XOR -- and it makes the S lane mirror the
  frame: A_n sits at the state that is C_n's frame bit, so the signalling
  mux and demux share the frame mux's address map.  Its price is the
  residual: speech satisfies two states 4 apart more often than 2 apart,
  so the chance acquisition of `seats` 3 is 2.4x as frequent as with
  3,5 -- and released by the guard counter either way.

  3,5 was chosen when S sat beside F.  A half-superframe shift maps 3 and
  7 onto each other, and with every channel off-hook the S lane has one
  zero and impersonates F at exactly that shift; the guard then read C1,
  and one data card in seat 1 false-locked (1 / 512) under 3,7.  With S
  half a frame from F that shift reads the F lane, not C1, and is dead for
  any guard (`mf8.py sbit`), which is what freed the choice.

S POSITION -- why S sits half a frame from F, not beside it

  The check reads two bits, F and S.  At a wrong bit alignment they land
  on two other lanes, SBIT apart.  A wrong alignment is a HOLE when both
  of those lanes carry fixed content that satisfies the check: the framer
  acquires it, the check never fails, and nothing can release it.
  Analytically, over every byte a data card could carry, in every seat,
  under all 64 hook states, with CVSD idle elsewhere:

  S at bit 1, beside F      [F][S][C1][C2][C3][C4][C5][C6]
    one data card, bytes that hold a wrong alignment:  seat 1: 64  seat 2: 0  seat 3: 0  seat 4: 0  seat 5: 0  seat 6: 1
      seat 1: 0x04 0x05 0x06 0x07 0x0C 0x0D 0x0E 0x0F ...
      seat 6: 0xFB
    two data cards, the F word in one and every byte in the other:
      unsafe pairs  (1,2) (1,3) (1,4) (1,5) (1,6) (2,3) (2,6) (3,4) (3,6) (4,5) (4,6) (5,6)
      safe pairs    (2,4) (2,5) (3,5)

  S at bit 4, opposite F    [F][C1][C2][C3][S][C4][C5][C6]
    one data card, bytes that hold a wrong alignment:  seat 1: 0  seat 2: 0  seat 3: 0  seat 4: 0  seat 5: 0  seat 6: 0
    two data cards, the F word in one and every byte in the other:
      unsafe pairs  (1,4) (2,5) (3,6)
      safe pairs    (1,2) (1,3) (1,5) (1,6) (2,3) (2,4) (2,6) (3,4) (3,5) (4,5) (4,6) (5,6)

  The framer confirms the holes S beside F has, all 64 hook states x 8 skews:
    seat 1 = 0x04, rest idle:  1 / 512 false locks, 1 held to the end
    seat 6 = 0xFB, rest idle:  448 / 512 false locks, 448 held to the end

  With S beside F, skew 1 reads S as F and C1 as the guard, and skew 7
  reads C6 as F and the NEXT frame's F as the guard.  Each is one data
  card plus the header, and the header is fixed content.  Seat 6: one
  byte passes for the word while the real F lane, at a fixed offset, reads
  exactly the guard's values.  Seat 1: with every channel off-hook S has
  one zero and passes for the word, and the guard then wants two
  particular bits of C1 -- 64 bytes.  Which bytes depends on the guard:
  with the guard at 3,5, as first found, they were 0x7F -- G.711 silence
  -- and 0x40; every guard has its own pair.  Both are deterministic, and
  both passed the old battery, whose "worst content" was one fixed
  pattern at one alignment.  Any pair that includes seat 1 or 6 inherits
  the hole, which is why only three pairs were actually safe.

  With S half a frame from F, the only skew that puts a header bit on a
  header position is 4, where the receiver reads S as F and F as S: S
  passes for the word only with every channel off-hook, and the F lane
  then reads 1,1 where the guard wants 0,1.  Every other skew reads two
  payload lanes three apart, so the slot rule becomes "not three apart".
  4 is the only spacing in an 8-bit frame that maps the header onto itself.

SLOT RULES -- what a data card may sit where

  1. Exhaustive and analytic: every byte, every seat, every hook state,
     CVSD idle elsewhere.  A "hold" is a wrong alignment the check passes
     for a whole superframe, which on periodic lanes is forever.

     one data card, bytes that hold:  seat 1: 0  seat 2: 0  seat 3: 0  seat 4: 0  seat 5: 0  seat 6: 0
     two data cards, the F word in one and every byte in the other:
       seats 1,4: holds, 8320 (byte, hook state) combinations
       seats 2,5: holds, 8320 (byte, hook state) combinations
       seats 3,6: holds, 8320 (byte, hook state) combinations
       safe: (1,2) (1,3) (1,5) (1,6) (2,3) (2,4) (2,6) (3,4) (3,5) (4,5) (4,6) (5,6)

  2. The framer, all 64 hook states x 8 skews (512 start-ups).  "false"
     is a false acquisition; "held" is one still wrong at the end.  The
     guard pattern is 0x08, the byte that satisfies the guard in phase.

  layout                                                false         held
    seat 1 = F word, rest voice                       0 / 512      0 / 512
    seat 2 = F word, rest voice                       0 / 512      0 / 512
    seat 3 = F word, rest voice                       0 / 512      0 / 512
    seat 4 = F word, rest voice                       0 / 512      0 / 512
    seat 5 = F word, rest voice                       0 / 512      0 / 512
    seat 6 = F word, rest voice                       0 / 512      0 / 512
    seats 1,4 = F word, guard pattern; rest voice    64 / 512     64 / 512
    seats 2,5 = F word, guard pattern; rest voice   128 / 512    128 / 512
    seats 3,6 = F word, guard pattern; rest voice   192 / 512    192 / 512
    seats 1,2 = F word, guard pattern; rest voice     0 / 512      0 / 512
    seats 3,4 = F word, guard pattern; rest voice     0 / 512      0 / 512
    seats 5,6 = F word, guard pattern; rest voice     0 / 512      0 / 512
    six data cards, random content                    0 / 512      0 / 512

  3. The residual: the F word in seat 1 and VOICE in seat 4, three apart.
     The guard lands on voice, which satisfies it by chance, and the
     framer acquires when that happens 4 superframes running.  Speech
     at nominal line level here (0.15 rms, as mf8_line.py), because the
     quieter lane the rest of this battery uses is closer to idle.
     Over 30 s of speech that is once in 499 attempts at that skew.  Forced at
     the first 24 such points, 0.5 s runs:

     tracking                                    acquired  released, frames     mean  never
     frame counter only                             24/24                 -        -     24
     frame counter + guard counter (this spec)      24/24            34-170  1.29 ms      0

  THE RULE.  Two data cards may not sit three slots apart: not (1,4),
  not (2,5), not (3,6).  One data card is safe in any seat whatever it
  carries, adjacent data cards are safe, ordinary random data is safe in
  all six seats, and three data cards fit as 1,2,3 or 4,5,6.  A data card
  carrying the F word three seats from VOICE is acquired by chance about
  once in 499 attempts at that skew, and the guard counter releases it
  within milliseconds, where the frame counter alone never would: with F
  always passing the guard is 2 checks in 8, and +2/-1 needs a third.

